{"step_index":388,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-04T06:01:39Z","input_tokens":2517,"cache_read_tokens":142473,"output_tokens":1879,"thinking":"The timeout error, specifically `TIMEOUT_CACHED_PIPE`, seems significant. The `fastboot devices` command unexpectedly still listed the device after the reboot command was issued. This implies a potential lingering fastboot connection even after a reboot.\n\n\nThe timeout stems from a persistent fastboot connection after reboot due to a missing USB disconnect packet from the U-Boot reboot command. This behavior, combined with the Android configuration `otg_device=0`, prevents re-enumeration and causes the ghost device listing in `fastboot devices`. The script's `fastboot reboot` command in U-Boot immediately resets the SoC into Android, confirming the issue's source.\n\n\nThe stale PID descriptor, `PID_0D02`, is likely cached by Windows Device Manager, causing the `fastboot devices` command to hang. Without a timeout, `fastboot.exe` hangs indefinitely when encountering a stale descriptor. The fix requires all calls to `fastboot.exe` to use a timeout wrapper to prevent indefinite hangs, similar to the approach used in `MOTOR_FASTBOOT_B866V2.ps1`. Exploring methods to clear the stale `PID_0D02` or detect `TIMEOUT_CACHED_PIPE` is the next logical step.\n\n\nThe ghost USB device is confirmed; `pnputil` offers a possible resolution by resetting the USB device node. Alternatively, instructing the user to re-enumerate the USB device could also resolve the issue.\n\nThe \"locked device\" failure requires tracing the user's Fastboot entry steps to understand the lock's state and how to unlock the device. Examining a provided screenshot will reveal the unlock mechanism.\n\n\nThe issue stems from the USB drive being present during boot, causing U-Boot to load Android Recovery (`PID_4EE7`) instead of Amlogic WorldCup. The script then immediately attempts a `fastboot reboot bootloader` which aims to reset to `PID_0D02` (U-Boot Fastboot), but this quick jump could be an issue. Reviewing the `Android Recovery` menu options, particularly \"Reboot to bootloader,\" in conjunction with the logs is crucial. This helps clarify the Fastboot entry method and if it is creating conflicting bootloader commands.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Select-String -Path \\\"c:\\\\Users\\\\andre\\\\OneDrive\\\\Desktop\\\\xview\\\\experimento\\\\*\\\\*.txt\\\",\\\"c:\\\\Users\\\\andre\\\\OneDrive\\\\Desktop\\\\xview\\\\experimento\\\\*\\\\*.md\\\" -Pattern \\\"Set USB device|Apply update from|Enter fastboot\\\" | Select-Object -First 30\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Check Recovery menu options documented in experimento\""}}]}
